From a734c549de88e01499b1e785abd3451232e6ad13 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Luuk van Dijk <lvd@mndmttr.net>
Date: Mon, 13 Jul 2026 23:13:25 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] NucleoF767ZI bootloader: two first-boot bugs, found with
 a debugger
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

FLASH_RESERVE_START_KB 0 in hwdef-bl: the generator default of 16
linked the bootloader at +16K while it was flashed at 0x08000000 —
the vector table pointed 16K past the code and the first instruction
after reset was erased flash (UNDEFINSTR HardFault, read off the
stacked exception frame).

AP_Bootloader's own strlen/memset get empty asm barriers in their
loops: gcc 15's loop-idiom recognition compiled strlen's body into a
tail call to strlen — itself ('strlen: b.w strlen'), an infinite loop
during USB descriptor setup, before enumeration and before the
boot-the-app timeout could ever run. Harmless on the pinned gcc 10;
this applies to any board built with a newer toolchain and is worth
upstreaming.

With both fixed: bootloader enumerates on CN13, times out, boots the
app; ArduPlane runs and presents SERIAL0 on the same port.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 Tools/AP_Bootloader/support.cpp               |  13 +++++++++++--
 Tools/bootloaders/NucleoF767ZI_bl.bin         | Bin 14156 -> 14172 bytes
 .../hwdef/NucleoF767ZI/hwdef-bl.dat           |   8 +++++++-
 3 files changed, 18 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Tools/AP_Bootloader/support.cpp b/Tools/AP_Bootloader/support.cpp
index c112a51773..b42f9d5206 100644
--- a/Tools/AP_Bootloader/support.cpp
+++ b/Tools/AP_Bootloader/support.cpp
@@ -425,20 +425,29 @@ int strcmp(const char *s1, const char *s2)
     return (*s1 - *s2);
 }
 
-//simple variant of std c function to reduce used flash space
+//simple variant of std c function to reduce used flash space.
+// The empty asm in the loop stops the compiler's loop-idiom recognition
+// from turning the body into a call to strlen — i.e. to itself, an
+// infinite loop (observed with gcc 15: 'strlen: b.w strlen').
 size_t strlen(const char *s1)
 {
     size_t ret = 0;
-    while (*s1++) ret++;
+    while (*s1++) {
+        ret++;
+        __asm__ volatile("");
+    }
     return ret;
 }
 
 //simple variant of std c function to reduce used flash space
+// (same idiom-recognition guard as strlen: gcc can turn this loop
+// into a call to memset)
 void *memset(void *s, int c, size_t n)
 {
     uint8_t *b = (uint8_t *)s;
     while (n--) {
         *b++ = c;
+        __asm__ volatile("");
     }
     return s;
 }
